                <pb facs="0081" n="45" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v11/pg45"/>

                <head>21. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>During the early months of 1964, U.S. Government agencies concerned with arms
                    control policy continued to develop a proposal for a strategic nuclear delivery
                    vehicles freeze. Regarding some of these discussions, see <ref target="#d2"
                        >Documents 2</ref>, <ref target="#d14">14</ref>, and <ref target="#d19"
                        >19</ref>. Additional documentation on formal papers and comments on them
                    can be found in the Johnson Library, National Security File, Subject File,
                    Disarmament, Vol. I, Boxes 10-11.</p>
                <p>Finally, in an April 14 memorandum to McGeorge
                        Bundy, Spurgeon Keeny
                    reported that the principals had cleared instructions for presentation of the
                    U.S. initiative to the Eighteen-Nation Disarmament Committee in Geneva. A typed
                    copy of the instructions is attached to Keeny’s memorandum. (Ibid., National Security File, Subject
                    File, Disarmament, Vol. I, Cables, Box 11) These instructions were transmitted
                    in Todis 1399, April 14. (Department of State,
                    Central Files, DEF 18-3)</p>
                <p>On April 16, Acting Representative Adrian
                        Fisher introduced this initiative in the Eighteen-Nation
                    Disarmament Committee. For text of his statement, see <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Documents on Disarmament, 1964</hi>, pages 157-162.</p>
            </div>
